eed then he  would have to uphold the mandate if the  mandate goes through it passes  constitutional muster does that mean  Congress would have the ability or the  government would have the ability to  make us eat broccoli to not eat broccoli  to chew broccoli a certain amount of  times before swallowing it  or is that all hyperbole I don't think  it's hyperbole I think that it would  then  be a political matter that's the real  difference here is whether there's a  constitutional restraint on what  Congress can do or whether it's all just  politics and if the court says that yes  the federal government can compel you to  buy health insurance then it would be a  matter of politics whether or not  Congress chooses to force you to buy a  Chevy Volt or buy whatever else they  think is good for society are good for  the economy or whatever a politician  thinks is a good idea what do you think  about the Medicaid expansion is that  going to go yes or no I think it's a  strong argument that the states have the  states are deprived of all Medicaid  funding if they don't comply the federal  government has never done something like  this before where it's expanded  eligibility requirements so dramatically  and although lower courts kind of have  all rejected this argument so far so far  I think that this case presents such an  extreme set of circumstances there was a  very famous case called South Dakota  versus Dole where the Supreme Court said  you know that's Elizabeth Dole right  Reagan's transportation circuit from  1987 on the South the state of South  Dakota was required to comply with  certain requirements in order to get a  certain percentage of its highway  funding the total amount of money at  issue in that case I believe is about  five million dollars and the court said  'no this the Congress can do that but  here we're talking I mean California  alone will lose twenty six billion  dollars if it doesn't comply and of  course would still be required to send  its taxes elsewhere more importantly  states are not allowed to opt out of  Medicaid as you know reason published an  article recently pointing out that under  the emergency treat medical treatment  act a federal statute also known as  EMTALA states are not allowed at this  point opt out of Medicaid and provide  alternative methods of taking care of  the poor a final question about  Obamacare issues if part of the law gets  struck down will that end the whole  thing or how will that plan that's  another issue on which the court will  hear an hour and a half of oral argument  on Wednesday I think that the court will  strike down the whole thing all at once  or not at all I don't think they're  going to get going to want to get into  the business of basically redesigning  the statute in a way that Congress never  intended sometimes they do that and they  could do that here there's no reason  that Congress couldn't that the court  couldn't just strike out the mandate  and leave the rest in place but to do so  would basically be rewriting the statute  and I don't think the courts gonna want  to do how on a scale of you know Dred  Scott versus Plessy versus Ferguson  Korematsu you know how important is this  as a Supreme Court case well it  certainly doesn't rate like Dred Scott  contrary to Professor Akhil amar  claiming that striking down the mandate  and upholding constitutional principle  was like Dred Scott but I think this  case is certainly the most important  case on the reach of federal power in 50  years and I think it's really amazing  that the court has granted three days of  oral arguments something it has not done  in at least a century I mean we're  talking about the days of Daniel Webster  here so I think the court is taking it  extremely seriously because although in  this case you're talking about just one  law that in the scheme of things doesn't  seem all that important the  constitutional principle of where does  the line where is the line drawn on  federal power that is a matter that our  children and grandchildren love to do  and let's also point out that they are  the Supreme Court justices are  government workers so when we say three  days what we're talking about is really  what a like a total of about six hours  that's right yeah okay spread out over  three days I'm the one that has to stand  in line for six hours now Pacific Legal  Foundation the nonprofit law firm that  you work for recently had a Supreme  Court victory in sac and V EPA quickly  what is the what's the import of that  decision that case involved the EPA's  use of what are called compliance orders  which are government orders issued by a  single bureaucrat on the basis of quote  any information whatsoever end quote  that declare you are out of compliance  of the Clean Water Act and force you to  come back into compliance by planting  all sorts of trees or roping off your  property all that sort of thing for  three years and if you don't comply you  get fined seventy five thousand dollars  per day is the total possible fines and  you're not allowed to sue the government  to challenge their jurisdiction what the  Supreme Court said in this case is that  under the under the Administrative  Procedure Act you do have the right to  sue and challenge the EPA's jurisdiction  so thanks to PLF the Sacketts now get to  go back to court and hopefully beat the  epa on the merits of the compliance  that's right and we're pretty optimistic  about that because  not only is their property perfectly dry  land but it's a normal everyday  subdivision there are built up  properties all around it and the federal  government clearly abused its power in  this case as it has done in so many  other cases where people didn't have the  wherewithal to defend themselves in  court  great well Timothy sandifer principal  attorney at the Pacific Legal Foundation  I want to thank you for talking to us  about Obamacare and the Supreme Court  for Reason TV I'm Nick Gillespie
 

